From 31ea13d09d984f2349ae91ef2c1b33af9c1b3472 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 13:22:22 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 8/8] build: LF in every checkout, so a Windows clone builds
 the same bytes

The release build is meant to be reproducible: the same commit and toolchain
give the same spklib-host.exe. On a Windows runner it did not match the
developer PC's: same size, 1,191 bytes different, all in .rsrc. Git for
Windows checks text out with CRLF (core.autocrlf=true), and
host/spklib.manifest is embedded in the exe as it is on disk, so the CRLF
checkout carried 23 more bytes of manifest. The same commit from an LF
checkout, built in a folder with another name, gave the PC's bytes exactly
(3956ba4a...), so nothing else in the binary depends on the machine.

.gitattributes now checks every text file out with LF (cmd scripts keep
CRLF, which cmd.exe needs). No file in the repository changes: they are all
LF already.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01EsiBMQLFocprcmVfDPxXCc
---
 .gitattributes | 4 ++++
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+)

diff --git a/.gitattributes b/.gitattributes
index 8517edc..5615c60 100644
--- a/.gitattributes
+++ b/.gitattributes
@@ -1,2 +1,6 @@
+# Every text file is checked out with LF on every machine, whatever core.autocrlf says: the
+# Windows build embeds host/spklib.manifest in the exe byte for byte, so a CRLF checkout would
+# build a different binary from the same commit. cmd.exe needs CRLF in its scripts.
+* text=auto eol=lf
 *.cmd text eol=crlf
 *.bat text eol=crlf
